  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1137 AM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nCutoff low will continue to spin over Baja California and SoCal \ntoday, trying to push in some moisture from the southwest but expect \ndry conditions today. Pressure gradient is still pretty tight along \nand west of the Continental Divide leading to some breezy conditions \nthis afternoon. Still keeping an eye out for dust on the Lordsburg \nPlaya but the cooler air will help keep it down but breezy winds \ncould create some dust. This closed low gets picked up and starts \nprogressing the trough towards our area Sunday. Low end showery \nactivity possible Sunday afternoon for western NM. A shortwave is \nwhat will be left of the upper low but looks to provide enough \nenergy and moisture to bring some showery activity overnight into \nMonday morning mostly focusing west of the Rio Grande Valley \n(RGV). PWs Monday and Tuesday will be well above average \n(0.8\"-0.9\") but instability parameters don't look very impressive \n(~200-500 J/kg). \n\nAnother upper low slides south off the west coast of the CONUS from \nthe Gulf of Alaska. By Wednesday, this closed low is off the coast \nof SoCal while there's a ridge just to our southeast. As time goes \non, expect this low to inch closer to make landfall near SoCal \nbefore lifting out to the northeast. This will allow for\nanomalously high moisture (PWs of an 1\" Wednesday, increasing to \n1.1\"-1.3\" Thursday) to push in. For reference, the average PW for \nmiddle of Oct is 0.5\", 90th percentile is 0.8\". and maximum values\nupwards of 1.15\". Wednesday and Thursday have the potential to \nbecome quite active with this upper trough. Instability values do \nlook better during this time, in addition to 0-6km shear values of\nanywhere from 20-50kt. Better shear looks to be focused out west \nduring this time. There will be a risk for flash flooding with \nthunderstorms, as well as some strong thunderstorms.",
